# ==================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES
#   `docker build` uploads a build CONTEXT to the daemon before the
#   first instruction runs. This file decides what never leaves the
#   working tree. Without it the context is 126 MB, of which 118 MB is
#   .git history and Maven output that no image layer could ever use.
#   With it the context is roughly 8 MB. That is a faster, and more
#   importantly a DETERMINISTIC, build: a context that carries target/
#   makes the result depend on whatever a previous local `mvn` run left
#   behind.
#
# DOCKERIGNORE IS NOT GITIGNORE - THE ONE THING TO GET RIGHT
#   The syntaxes look identical and behave differently, so the rules
#   here are deliberately NOT copied from .gitignore.
#
#   Every pattern is ANCHORED AT THE CONTEXT ROOT. In .gitignore a bare
#   `*.log` hides a log file at any depth; here it matches ./x.log and
#   nothing else. Depth-independent matching needs an explicit `**/`
#   prefix, which is why the metadata, editor and credential rules below
#   all carry one and the build-output rules deliberately do not.
#
#   That difference cuts both ways, and one case decides a real defect.
#   `target` without a prefix reaches ONLY the build directory at the
#   repository root - and that is exactly what is wanted, because
#   src/test/resources may hold parity baselines below a path component
#   of that name. Writing `**/target` would silently delete protected
#   fixture space from the context. pom.xml declares no <modules>, so
#   the build is single-module and the anchored form is complete as well
#   as safe. Should this ever become a multi-module build, add one
#   anchored rule per module rather than reaching for `**/`.
#
#   Docker also always sends Dockerfile and .dockerignore themselves,
#   whatever the rules say, so there is no rule for either.
#
# SCOPE AND PRECEDENCE
#   The LAST matching pattern wins, so a `!` negation must come after
#   the rule it re-includes. There is exactly ONE negation in this file
#   and it is live: `.env.*` genuinely matches .env.example, so
#   `!.env.example` is required to keep the template - which holds
#   variable names and empty placeholders only - in the context.
#
# WHAT MUST NEVER BE IGNORED
#   app/cbl, app/cpy, app/cpy-bms, app/bms, app/jcl, app/csd, app/ctl,
#   app/proc, app/catlg   the frozen COBOL corpus. It is at once the
#                         parity oracle, the field-contract source and
#                         the traceability anchor, and tests and
#                         traceability read these contracts.
#   app/data/ASCII/**     all NINE fixtures: acctdata.txt, carddata.txt,
#                         cardxref.txt, custdata.txt, dailytran.txt,
#                         discgrp.txt, tcatbal.txt, trancatg.txt and
#                         trantype.txt. dailytran.txt is the 300-record,
#                         350-byte-per-record Gate 1 fixture, and it
#                         carries both `{` and `}` overpunch signs, so
#                         it is the only input that exercises the
#                         cycle-debit branch of the posting logic. Note
#                         the spelling: the fixture is dailytran.txt in
#                         full, even though the mainframe DD name and
#                         dataset are DALYTRAN.
#   src/**                Java sources, resources, Flyway migrations,
#                         the validation JSON, and the fixtures and
#                         parity baselines under src/test/resources
#   pom.xml, mvnw, mvnw.cmd, .mvn/**        the build, wrapper-pinned
#   Dockerfile, docker-compose.yml          the topology
#   localstack-init/**, observability/**    provisioning
#   docs/**, DECISION_LOG.md, TRACEABILITY_MATRIX.md    gate evidence
#   .env.example                            names and placeholders only
#
#   No glob below matches a content format this repository ships. There
#   is deliberately no *.txt, *.md, *.json, *.sql, *.yml, *.html or
#   *.zip rule, and no bare `app`, `build` or `data` rule either.
#   Fourteen tracked .txt files are fixed-width parity fixtures and the
#   VSAM catalogue listing, four tracked .json files are application and
#   dashboard resources, and `data` is a real path component of
#   app/data. Any of those patterns would silently strip shipped
#   content. The extension globs that do appear cover only build output,
#   runtime files, private key material and editor state - none of which
#   this repository has ever tracked.
#
#   Forward compatibility matters as much as the current tree. Several
#   mandated artefacts are authored after this file - DECISION_LOG.md,
#   TRACEABILITY_MATRIX.md, the rest of docs/, the Flyway migrations and
#   the profile YAML among them. A broad rule that looks harmless today
#   would hide them on the day they land, silently and with no error.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED, AND WHY
#   diagrams/ and .github/. Neither is a build input, but neither is a
#   local-only artefact either: both are tracked, shared reference
#   material, and .github/workflows/build.yml is the pinned JDK 25 and
#   Maven 3.9.11 harness that the build-verification gate is proven
#   against. Excluding tracked shared content to save 1.2 MB would trade
#   a silent gate failure for a rounding error, so both stay.
#
#   .gitignore and .gitattributes. Also tracked shared configuration,
#   and .gitattributes is the record of which corpus members are
#   CRLF-terminated - information a fixed-width parity check may need.
#
#   Build reports. Every report this build emits already lands under
#   target/: pom.xml pins the JaCoCo and OWASP output to
#   ${project.build.directory} and Surefire and Failsafe default there.
#   Restating those paths would duplicate one rule and could only drift
#   out of step with it.
#
#   A wrapper jar. .mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties sets
#   distributionType=only-script, so no maven-wrapper.jar is ever
#   fetched into .mvn/wrapper and no rule is needed to hide one.
#
#   Other language stacks. This is a single-module Maven project with no
#   Gradle, Node or front-end build, and the AWS CLI virtualenv the
#   setup guide provisions lives outside the repository. Rules for
#   node_modules, .venv or __pycache__ would be dead on arrival.
#
# HOW TO VERIFY A CHANGE TO THIS FILE
#   Ask Docker itself rather than reasoning about the patterns; the
#   anchoring differences above are too easy to get wrong by eye.
#
#     printf 'FROM alpine:3.17\nCOPY . /ctx\nRUN find /ctx -type f\n' \
#       > /tmp/ctx.Dockerfile
#     DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0 docker build --no-cache -f /tmp/ctx.Dockerfile .
#
#   The listing it prints IS the context. Every path named under WHAT
#   MUST NEVER BE IGNORED above must appear in it, and .git, target,
#   samples and app/data/EBCDIC must not. Then confirm the real build
#   still resolves every COPY:
#
#     docker build --no-cache -t carddemo:verify .
#
# FAILURE MODES TO AVOID
#   Never add a glob for a format this repository ships, never add a
#   bare `app`, and never convert an anchored build-output rule to a
#   `**/` form. Each failure is SILENT: docker build prints nothing,
#   the file simply is not in the context, and the first symptom is a
#   fixture-not-found test failure or a gate that cannot be evidenced -
#   attributed to the test rather than to this file. Re-run the context
#   listing above after any edit; it is the only check that catches this
#   class of mistake.
# ==================================================================

# ---- Version-control history. 28 MB of packfiles that no image layer
# ---- can use. Safe to drop because no Maven plugin in this build reads
# ---- git metadata: there is no git-commit-id or buildnumber plugin, so
# ---- nothing derives a version or build stamp from .git.
.git

# ---- Maven build output. Anchored ON PURPOSE, for the reason given at
# ---- length above: this is the one build directory, it covers the
# ---- packaged jar, the compiled classes and every generated report,
# ---- and the anchored form cannot reach a same-named path component
# ---- under src/test/resources. Dropping it also keeps a stale local
# ---- jar from ever masking a fresh in-image build.
target

# ---- IntelliJ IDEA's own compiler output, written to the project root
# ---- when the IDE builds in place instead of delegating to Maven.
out

# ---- A stray class file left beside a source by an ad-hoc `javac`
# ---- invocation. It lands outside target/, so the rule above does not
# ---- cover it, and it can land at any depth in the source tree.
**/*.class

# ---- JVM fatal-error, replay and heap-dump files. The JVM writes these
# ---- into the working directory, which for a forked Surefire JVM is
# ---- not necessarily the project root, so these are depth-independent.
**/hs_err_pid*
**/replay_pid*
**/*.hprof

# ---- Local runtime output from a developer's own `mvn spring-boot:run`
# ---- or `java -jar`. The application logs to stdout in a container, so
# ---- anything matching here is by definition host-local. The logs
# ---- directory is anchored: a nested one would belong to whatever
# ---- produced it and is covered by the wildcard rules instead.
logs
**/*.log
**/*.pid
**/*.tmp

# ---- Local environment overrides. `.env.*` also matches the template
# ---- .env.example, which .env.example itself declares must stay
# ---- available ("Copy it to .env, which is git-ignored"), so the
# ---- negation that follows is required rather than defensive - it is
# ---- the only negation in this file. The JWT signing key is
# ---- environment-indirected with no committed default, so a
# ---- developer's real values live only in .env and must never be
# ---- uploaded to the daemon or baked into a layer.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ---- Private key material and credential stores. These are preventive
# ---- controls: nothing in this repository matches one today, and that
# ---- is the point - the value is that they fire before the accident,
# ---- keeping a key a developer dropped into the tree out of the
# ---- context and therefore out of the image. Public certificate
# ---- formats are deliberately absent, so a public test certificate
# ---- added later stays available. id_rsa and id_ed25519 are matched
# ---- exactly, not as id_rsa*, so a .pub half is not caught too. All
# ---- are depth-independent, because a leaked credential is a hazard
# ---- wherever it lands and no deliverable carries any of these names.
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/id_rsa
**/id_ed25519
**/.aws
**/credentials
**/secrets

# ---- IDE and editor state, all of it user-local. Unlike .gitignore,
# ---- which keeps the three shareable .vscode team files by negation,
# ---- the whole directory goes here: none of those files is a build
# ---- input, so there is nothing for a negation to rescue and adding
# ---- one would only imply protection it does not provide.
**/.idea
**/*.iml
**/*.ipr
**/*.iws
**/.vscode
**/.classpath
**/.project
**/.settings
**/.factorypath
**/.apt_generated

# ---- Editor swap and backup files, and the residue `git merge` and
# ---- `git apply` leave behind on a conflict. A .orig or .rej left in
# ---- the tree would otherwise be uploaded and could be copied into a
# ---- layer alongside the file it shadows.
**/*.swp
**/*.swo
**/*~
**/*.bak
**/*.orig
**/*.rej

# ---- Operating-system metadata. macOS first, then Windows. Also
# ---- preventive: these are created by a file browser, not by this
# ---- project, and appear at arbitrary depth when they appear at all.
**/.DS_Store
**/._*
**/.Spotlight-V100
**/.Trashes
**/Thumbs.db
**/desktop.ini

# ---- Platform scratch: screenshots and screen recordings, and the
# ---- ad-hoc validation scripts that must never reach a commit, let
# ---- alone an image layer.
blitzy
**/blitzy_adhoc_test_*

# ==================================================================
# OUT OF SCOPE FOR THE IMAGE BUILD - THE ONLY TWO CONTENT TREES DROPPED
#
# These two are the single point where this file deliberately diverges
# from .gitignore, which keeps both. The two files answer different
# questions. .gitignore decides what reaches a CLONE, and in a clone
# these must survive byte-for-byte as the reference of record.
# .dockerignore decides what reaches the BUILD DAEMON, and the migration
# plan is explicit that the Java build must never parse either of them.
#
# Directory form is used rather than a trailing `/**` so the daemon
# prunes each subtree without descending into it.
# ==================================================================

# ---- z/OS build tooling: three compile templates, three build
# ---- procedures, and two binary emulator runtime archives under
# ---- samples/m2 that together account for 902 KB of the 930 KB tree.
# ---- Nothing here has a Java analogue; pom.xml supersedes the set.
# ---- Excluding the directory is also what makes a `*.zip` rule
# ---- unnecessary - and a `*.zip` rule is exactly the kind that would
# ---- later hide these two archives from a clone if copied across.
samples

# ---- The EBCDIC datasets: twelve fixed-width .PS files retained as
# ---- byte-level codepage reference only. No transcoding is performed
# ---- and no codepage conversion utility is built, so the build never
# ---- reads them. The ASCII fixtures under app/data/ASCII are the
# ---- authoritative seed and test input and are deliberately untouched
# ---- by this rule - note that it names the EBCDIC directory
# ---- specifically and never app/data, which would take both.
app/data/EBCDIC

# ==================================================================
# EVERYTHING ELSE IS PROTECTED BY OMISSION, NOT BY NEGATION
#
# app/ (less the EBCDIC datasets above), src/, docs/, diagrams/,
# observability/, localstack-init/, .github/, .mvn/, pom.xml, mvnw,
# mvnw.cmd, Dockerfile, docker-compose.yml and the evidence artefacts
# all stay in the context, and they stay there because no rule above
# reaches them - not because a `!` line rescues them.
#
# That is a deliberate choice, and it mirrors how .gitignore protects
# the same corpus. A line such as `!app/**` would read as protection
# while doing nothing at all: a negation only takes effect against an
# earlier pattern that matches, and no pattern above matches anything
# under app/cbl, app/cpy, app/cpy-bms, app/bms, app/jcl, app/csd,
# app/ctl, app/proc, app/catlg or app/data/ASCII. Adding one would be
# dead weight of exactly the kind that misleads the next reader into
# thinking the guarantee is enforced here when it is not.
#
# The guarantee is asserted by test instead, and the test is the
# context listing given under HOW TO VERIFY A CHANGE above. It walks
# the context Docker actually built and must show every mandated path
# present. Any absence is a defect in this file.
#
# One case is worth stating explicitly because a wildcard makes it easy
# to lose. app/jcl holds 29 members and 28 of them end in a lowercase
# .jcl, while CREASTMT.JCL - the sole source for statement generation -
# ends in an uppercase .JCL. Docker matches case-sensitively, so any
# pattern written as app/jcl/*.jcl would treat the two differently.
# There is no rule here that touches app/jcl at all, which is the only
# form of that guarantee that cannot be got wrong.
# ==================================================================
